ion of the curve by its arc length $s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120279 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd2d22fe-d6c9-55e5-8f32-f782e37e2d9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120279 #edb/ref 1074 "First, since $s=7t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{7}.$\n\nThe vector function that pametrizes the curve $r(t)$ by its arc length $s$, with $s\\ge 0$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{7}) \\\\  & =⟨2(\\frac{s}{7}),\\,1-3(\\frac{s}{7}),\\,5+6(\\frac{s}{7})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}⟨2s,\\,7-3s,\\,35+6s⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120287 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120287 #edb/ref 1069 "q-33974" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120287 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120287 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the parametrization of the curve $r(t)=⟨3t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ by its arc length $s?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120287 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf7d29a0-f7b5-5ec6-9f09-d4bee4934cb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120287 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16({cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t))} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5u\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =5t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s=5t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{5}) \\\\  & =⟨3\\cdot \\frac{s}{5},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{s}{5}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{s}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3s}{5},\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120576 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120576 #edb/ref 1061 10294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120576 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3cac974a-1992-543d-93ed-f87c6e569e32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120576 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120577 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120577 #edb/ref 1061 10295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120577 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "316716f0-90d4-5cb3-8194-1e5c03222fc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120577 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120578 #edb/ref 1061 10298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120578 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d0612bf4-b80b-510a-873d-f90bcf69f311" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120578 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120579 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120579 #edb/ref 1061 10301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120579 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "beb05cc6-e9de-51c2-91d9-49857b9e53c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120579 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121001 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: \\sqrt{5} t\\rangle, \\qquad t \\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nFor any $t >0,$ the arc length $s$ of $C$ from $\\mathbf r(0)$ to $\\mathbf r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡u+{sin}^{2}⁡u)+5}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{9}\\,du \\\\  & =3u{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $s= 3t,$ or equivalently $t = \\dfrac{s}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we can define a new vector function $\\mathbf{R}$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(s)= \\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) = \\left\\langle 2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: 2\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: \\sqrt{5} \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) \\right\\rangle .\n$$\n\nThe function $\\mathbf{R}(s)$ gives us the *same* curve $C,$ but this time it is parameterized by the arc length $s.$\n\nIn general, if $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$ is a continuously differentiable vector function such that $\\mathbf r'(t )\\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then to parametrize it in terms of arc length we perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Compute $s = \\displaystyle s(t) = \\int_a^t\\|\\mathbf r'(u)\\| \\, \\mathrm{d}u$\n\n2\\. Write $s = s(t)$ in the form $t=t(s)$\n\n3\\. Substitute $t=t(s)$ back into $\\mathbf r(t)$ to get $\\mathbf R(s)=\\mathbf r(t(s))$\n\n**Note:** One advantage of parametrizing a curve by its arc length is that the tangent vector is always a unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\left\\|\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf R}{\\textrm{d} s}\\right\\| = 1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121001 #edb/ref 1065 2375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121001 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a2ecbf26-2299-5d4f-9ca0-ac626810c1ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121001 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260006 #edb/ref 1080 "(s+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s)]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260006 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260006 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e1fe70b-f9e8-5e14-95bd-936e4e6cefc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186260078 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(s