nd ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3-{t}^{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3},\\,2{t}^{3},\\,-4{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}⟨2,\\,1,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =|2{t}^{3}|\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}\\sqrt{4+1+4} \\\\  & =6{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}6{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{t}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{4}$ and $t\\ge 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{4} & =s \\\\ {t}^{4} & =\\frac{2s}{3} \\\\ t & =\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{2s}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{2s}{3}}) \\\\  & =⟨{(\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{2s}{3}})}^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2}{(\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{2s}{3}})}^{4}+2,\\,3-{(\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{2s}{3}})}^{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2s}{3},\\,\\frac{s}{3}+2,\\,3-\\frac{2s}{3}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2s,\\,s+6,\\,9-2s⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312004 #edb/ref 1080 "R(s)=\\frac{1}{7}⟨2s,\\,1-3s,\\,5+6s⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312004 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186312004 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04e527b9-3209-50d5-aaa3-8bf133b7f3dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313051 #edb/ref 1080 "R(s)=⟨2s,\\,7+3s,\\,35+6s⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313051 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313051 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "860b9d1d-8a3f-5f6e-aa07-757fcfca11c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186313215 #edb/ref 1080 "R(s)=\\frac{1}{7}⟨2s,\\,7+3s,\\,35+6s⟩" #edb/ref 1759218