] [17592186348078 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78390" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348078 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348078 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the parametrization of the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{2},\\,\\frac{t}{2},\\,t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ by its arc length $s?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348078 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec5db497-36ab-565c-9851-a44645728973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348078 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\cdot u{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\,t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\,t$,  we have that $t=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,s.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,s,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,s,\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,s⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{6},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}\\,s}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348079 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348079 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78403" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348079 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348079 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve  $r(t)=⟨2{e}^{t},\\,3{e}^{t},\\,6{e}^{t}⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=7({e}^{t}-1),$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348079 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "80d24151-001d-558c-b4cf-13fe29c86835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348079 #edb/ref 1074 "First, since $s=7({e}^{t}-1)$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{s}{7} & ={e}^{t}-1 \\\\ {e}^{t} & =\\frac{s}{7}+1 \\\\ t & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{s+7}{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{s+7}{7})) \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{s+7}{7})},\\,3{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{s+7}{7})},\\,6{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{s+7}{7})}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot \\frac{s+7}{7},\\,3\\cdot \\frac{s+7}{7},\\,6\\cdot \\frac{s+7}{7}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2s+14}{7},\\,\\frac{3s+21}{7},\\,\\frac{6s+42}{7}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2s+14}{7}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3s+21}{7}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{6s+42}{7})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\frac{3}{7},\\,\\frac{6}{7}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393849 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186393850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393849 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393849 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393849 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve  $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,4{t}^{2},\\,8{t}^{2}⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=9{t}^{2},$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393849 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56897adb-706a-5c81-8930-6f2aa15c0be7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393849 #edb/ref 1074 "First, since $s=9{t}^{2}$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{s}{9} \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3}) \\\\  & =⟨{(\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3})}^{2},\\,4{(\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3})}^{2},\\,8{(\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3})}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{s}{9},\\,\\frac{4s}{9},\\,\\frac{8s}{9}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{9}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4s}{9}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{8s}{9})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{4}{9},\\,\\frac{8}{9}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393850 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393850 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393850 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393850 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393850 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186393855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393850 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393850 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393850 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186393851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393850 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7fefcfb2-453a-5854-be55-e93605da6418" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393851 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{4}{9},\\,\\frac{8}{9}⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186393851 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921863